{
 "note": "extshape.py -- what SHAPE each GLSL.std.450 instruction lowers to.\n\nnotes/39 established the chain that names a builtin's GLASM OPCODE, and it\nalso established why the opcode alone is not enough to emit anything: cgc\nINLINES a builtin that has a body (`sign` becomes SLT/SGT/ADD/I2F, not `SSG`),\nand it SCALARISES the transcendentals (one instruction per component, not one\nwhole-vector instruction).  Emitting from the opcode alone got 6 probes wrong.\n\nThe missing fact is not in the opcode table; it is in what the front end\nbuilds.  `g2s_trace_fold` dumps every node of the emit list with its opcode and\nits destination mask (notes/49), so running the oracle answers it directly:\n\n    one node, mask 0xffffffff   -> ONE whole-vector instruction\n    four nodes, masks .x        -> scalarised, one per component\n    several unrelated opcodes   -> the builtin was inlined\n\nThis writes that measurement to `notes/extinst_shape.json`, with the mnemonic\nthe image's namer table gives for the measured opcode and the opcode the\nnotes/39 chain independently predicts, so the two can be compared.  The\nemitter consults it and refuses anything that is not `single`.\n\nTHIS IS A MEASUREMENT OF THE COMPILER, NOT OF A LISTING.  The opcode and the\nmask come out of the front end's own nodes; the mnemonic and the type suffix\ncome out of the image's tables.  No listing text is read.\n",
